<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benin Bronzes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1485">1485</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronzes</a> are thousands of cast metal plaques, heads and sculptures, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and other objects, made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> working for the royal court of the Benin Empire. The site dates the tradition to 1485–1897, from early contact with the Portuguese to the British expedition that looted them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>. Celebrated as masterpieces of African art, they are held largely in Western museums and are the subject of ongoing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a> debates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes.jpg" alt="Benin Bronzes" width="302" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benin_brass_plaque_03_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
